Example of the JSON shape, one confirmed and one refuted outcome:
{"outcomes": [
  {"unit_id": "threat-t8", "verdict": "confirmed",
   "rationale": "The bearer token in core/config.py is a single shared secret with no per-caller identity; ALREADY IN PLACE only checks that the token is set, not who holds it.",
   "findings": [
     {"cwe": "CWE-306", "file": "routers/repo_credentials.py",
      "line_start": 42, "line_end": 58, "repeat_lines": [71, 96], "sink_line": 0,
      "title": "Missing per-caller authentication", "severity": "high",
      "description": "...", "exploit_scenario": "...", "recommendation": "...",
      "preconditions": ["Caller needs no session or bearer token — this route accepts anonymous requests"]}
   ]},
  ^ line_start/line_end, sink_line and repeat_lines: the schema field descriptions are authoritative. Here 42-58 spans the whole construct (decorator to end of body); sink_line is 0 because a missing control has no single dangerous statement (a query executed at line 55 would be "sink_line": 55); repeat_lines [71, 96] reports the SAME defect at two more sites with the prose written once — never one 42-96 span swallowing all three. preconditions and exploit_scenario are likewise governed by their own schema descriptions — name the gate (unauthenticated reach, a feature flag, a specific input shape), never leave preconditions empty by default, and let exploit_scenario reach only consequences the code you read supports.
  {"unit_id": "threat-t7", "verdict": "refuted",
   "rationale": "The timing difference in login() is under 2ms across 50 runs and does not correlate with account existence — not exploitable as a side channel.",
   "findings": []}
]}

